ne_margin": 8.98, "analyst_adjustment": -0.48, "key_factors": ["Open seat in a D+3 district after Rep. Don Bacon's retirement", "All nine expert ratings call a Democratic flip, from Tilt D to Safe D", "Prediction markets price Powell at 93-94% to win", "Powell leads in money, with about $3.1M in outside support versus essentially none for Harding"], "rationale": "Bacon's retirement leaves a D+3 open seat in a favorable environment; raters unanimously call a flip, markets price Powell near 93%, and her fundraising and outside-spending edge outweigh a single September poll that showed a narrower four-point race.", "watch": "A strong Harding debate performance or a late shift on affordability could tighten the race, since the only poll conducted showed a four-point margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Don Bacon (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Don Bacon's retirement turns Nebraska's 2nd District, a D+3 Omaha-area seat, into an open race that raters uniformly expect to flip. All nine outlets rate it a Democratic gain, from Tilt D to Safe D, and prediction markets put Denise Powell's chances near 93-94%. Powell won a contested May primary, outraised Brinker Harding, and benefits from roughly $3.1 million in outside support, while Harding shows no reported outside spending. A lone September SurveyUSA poll of likely voters had Powell up four points, closer than the broader signals.\n\nThe district's lean and a favorable national environment point toward Democrats, though the single poll and a Libertarian on the ballot leave some room. Harding has about $1.1 million on hand, and scheduled debates in October will give both campaigns a stage. The evidence favors a Democratic pickup, with the main open question being the size of the margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9278, "margin": 8.8}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9035, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.914, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem"